$\begin{aligned} \mathbf{M}&= \begin{pmatrix} c_{11}&c_{12} \\ c_{21}&c_{22} \end{pmatrix},\\ \\ \mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} &=c_{11}+c_{12}\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 21 \end{pmatrix}\\ c_{11}+3c_{12}&=6\\ -c_{11}+c_{12}&=0,\\ c_{11}+3c_{12}&=6\\ \\ \mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} &=c_{21}+c_{22}\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -17 \end{pmatrix}\\ c_{21}-c_{22}&=-3\\ -c_{21}+5c_{22}&=-17. \end{aligned}$
Adding the first row and second row, we have
\[\begin{pmatrix} c_{11} & c_{12} \\ c_{21} & c_{22}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 \\ 0 & 5\end{pmatrix}= \begin{pmatrix} 6 & -1 \\ 3 & -17\end{pmatrix}.\]
And dividing by 6, we have
\[\begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{3} \\ \frac{1}{2} & \frac{17}{6}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}.\]From here, we get
\[\mathbf{M}=\begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{3} \\ \frac{1}{2} & \frac{17}{6}\end{pmatrix}.\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{3} \\ \frac{1}{2} & \frac{17}{6}\end{pmatrix}}.\] [asy]
/* AMC8 Problems 20-25 solution */
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(9));
pen fixpen = fontsize(8);
pen del = darkgrey;

/* Construct axes */
draw((-6,0)--(6,0), del);
draw((0,-6)--(0,6), del);
draw((0,0)--dir(45), fixpen);

/* Draw triangles T and T' */
draw((1,-sqrt(63)/3)--(2,-sqrt(63)/3), del);
draw((3,sqrt(63)/3)--(4,sqrt(63)/3), del);
draw((5,-sqrt(63)/3)--(6,-sqrt(63)/3), del);
draw((1,sqrt(63)/3)--(3,-sqrt(63)/3), fixpen);
draw((2,sqrt(63)/3)--(4,-sqrt(63)/3), fixpen);
draw((3,0)--(3,6), fixpen);

/* Superimpose the matrix */
draw((-1,-1)--(5,-1));
draw((-1,sqrt(63)/3)--(5,sqrt(63)/3), blue);
draw(shift((1,sqrt(63)/3))*scale(1/2,1/3)*shift(-1,sqrt(63)/3)*((0,0)--(1,0)--(0,1)--(-1,0)--cycle), fixpen);
draw(shift((5,sqrt(63)/3))*scale(1/2,17/6)*shift(-1,sqrt(63)/3)*((0,0)--(1,0)--(0,1)--(-1,0)--cycle), blue);
/* and point representation */
dot((0,0), black);
dot((1,-sqrt(63)/3), del);
dot((2,-sqrt(63)/3), del);
dot((3,-sqrt(63)/3), del);
dot((1,sqrt(63)/3), del);
dot((3,sqrt(63)/3), del);
dot((5,-sqrt(63)/3), del);
dot((6,-sqrt(63)/3), del);
dot((3,-sqrt(63)/3), del);
dot((5,sqrt(63)/3), del);
draw((1,sqrt(63)/3)--(2,sqrt(63)/3), blue);
draw((3,sqrt(63)/3)--(4,sqrt(63)/3), blue);draw((1,sqrt(63)/3)--(3,sqrt(63)/3), del);
draw((2,sqrt(63)/3)--(4,sqrt(63)/3), del);
label("$T$",dir(215));
label("$T'$",dir(4));
label("$M$",shift((1,-1.5))*dir(215));
label("$(3,-1)$", (2,-sqrt(63)/3), fixpen);
label("$(1,\sqrt{63}/3)$", (3,-sqrt(63)/3), fixpen);
label("$(5,-1)$", (5,-sqrt(63)/3), fixpen);
label("$(1,\sqrt{63}/3)$", (3,sqrt(63)/3), del);

[/asy]

Since $\mathbf{M}$ is an $2 \times 2$ matrix, the computations in the problem give us the following two $4 \times 1$ matrices: \[\mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{M}_{11} & \mathbf{M}_{12} \\ \mathbf{M}_{21} & \mathbf{M}_{22} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \mathbf{M}_{11} + 0 \cdot \mathbf{M}_{12} \\ 3 \mathbf{M}_{21} + 0 \cdot \mathbf{M}_{22} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \mathbf{M}_{11} \\ 3 \mathbf{M}_{21} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 21 \end{pmatrix}.\]For the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$, the computations give us the system of equations \begin{align*}
3 \mathbf{M}_{11} &= 6, \\
3 \mathbf{M}_{21} &= 21.
\end{align*}Solving, we find $\mathbf{M}_{11} = 2$ and $\mathbf{M}_{21} = 7.$Similarly, \[\mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{M}_{11} & \mathbf{M}_{12} \\ \mathbf{M}_{21} & \mathbf{M}_{22} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -\mathbf{M}_{11} + 5 \mathbf{M}_{12} \\ -\mathbf{M}_{21} + 5 \mathbf{M}_{22} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -\mathbf{M}_{11} + 5 \mathbf{M}_{12} \\ -\mathbf{M}_{21} + 5 \mathbf{M}_{22} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ -17 \end{pmatrix}.\]For the vector $\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -17 \end{pmatrix}$, the computations give us the system of equations \begin{align*}
-\mathbf{M}_{11} + 5 \mathbf{M}_{12} &= -3, \\
-\mathbf{M}_{21} + 5 \mathbf{M}_{22} &= -17.
\end{align*}Solving, we find $\mathbf{M}_{11} = 2$ and $\mathbf{M}_{21} = -7.$Therefore, the matrix $\mathbf{M}$ must be $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ -7 & 5 \end{pmatrix}}.$. The answer is \begin{pmatrix}2&0\-7&5\end{pmatrix}
